proto-issue: library is the knowledge kind — adopted from the triage ground and canonized

The constellation had a category for people writing (voices), for broadcast (media), for business (trade), for the civic node (city), and — once bottles arrived — for files. It had none for **knowledge**. That one is **library**, and naming it was most of the work.

Adopted out of the triage ground (`advocates/constellation.anecdote.channel/library-for-advocate-knowledge.md`, raised 2026-09-02) through the door the ground's own README names for design writing that is already shaped: a proto-issue here. Written as a new file, so it cannot conflict with the other open OPEN-QUESTIONS PRs.

**What canonizing it actually means, mechanically** — this is the part that wasn't in the original item:

- **A library is two positions, not one.** As a *category* it is an unserved intermediate hostname level, and `directory.mjs` already renders those for free ("unserved levels render as CATEGORIES; serving one later turns the same grouping into a listing without changing this file") — so it costs nothing to introduce, which is decent evidence the position was always real and merely unnamed. As a *role* it is a new entry in `ROLE_ORDER` beside `journal`/`tell`/`atlas`/`antidote`, and that is the part that has to be built.
- Singular on purpose: many libraries will exist, but the category reads as *the place*, the way `media` and `city` do.
- `library.anecdote.channel` as an engine in the journal-engine mold, mounting `.journal-engine` (it publishes), `.you-engine` (so a person can authorize against it) and `.advocate-engine` (it has seats).
- **The distinguishing property**: a library has its own enumeration mission. An Atlas has to build an index; a library is already listing what's in it, so a library plugged into an Atlas is that index on tap. That asymmetry is why it's a kind and not a folder.
- **The clerk seat**, whose job is explicitly *not* to write documentation that gets cited instead of the real thing — it synthesizes from what it observes and re-reads its own notes before speaking about a resource.
- **Submissions are lists, carried as data-bottles** (D17), which buys the thing that matters: the list stays rewritable by its owner after it is given away. Prune, annotate why something left, fold old reports into log-scaled summaries, roll back.
- **The "where do we submit the diffs?" question dissolves**: the library already holds the data-pile, so you bring a diff to a bottle already sitting in a pile it operates. That pushes the real question onto pile-level authorization, which is §AA (civic-node#125).

**Open list includes one reconciliation nobody has done**: unclaimed poll data currently has a decay ladder sketched for it — cold storage, publish the key at rest, then break the key and let it go loose. A library is plausibly the better alternative to that last step, but the two have never been put side by side.

**I did not delete the triage-ground file.** The ground's README says adoption clears it, and normally I'd follow that — but that folder is outside version control, so deleting is unrecoverable. It's marked `promoted →` this PR instead; say the word and I'll remove it.
